    "text": "To solve for \\( AC \\) in the given right triangle \\( ABC \\) with \\( \\angle ABC = 90^\\circ \\), \\( AB = 12 \\), and \\( BD = 4 \\), we will use the Angle Bisector Theorem and the Pythagorean Theorem.\n\nFirst, let's denote the lengths of the sides as follows:\n- \\( AB = c = 12 \\)\n- \\( BD = 4 \\)\n- \\( DC = x \\)\n- \\( BC = BD + DC = 4 + x \\)\n- \\( AC = b \\)\n\nAccording to the Angle Bisector Theorem, the angle bisector \\( AD \\) divides the opposite side \\( BC \\) into segments proportional to the adjacent sides \\( AB \\) and \\( BC \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{BD}{DC} = \\frac{AB}{AC}\n\\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{x} = \\frac{12}{b}\n\\]\n\nWe also know that \\( BC = BD + DC = 4 + x \\). Using the Pythagorean Theorem in triangle \\( ABC \\):\n\\[\nAC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( AB = 12 \\) and \\( BC = 4 + x \\):\n\\[\nb^2 = 12^2 + (4 + x)^2\n\\]\n\\[\nb^2 = 144 + (4 + x)^2\n\\]\n\\[\nb^2 = 144 + 16 + 8x + x^2\n\\]\n\\[\nb^2 = x^2 + 8x + 160\n\\]\n\nFrom the Angle Bisector Theorem equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{x} = \\frac{12}{b}\n\\]\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\\[\n4b = 12x\n\\]\n\\[\nb = 3x\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( b = 3x \\) into the Pythagorean Theorem equation:\n\\[\n(3x)^2 = x^2 + 8x + 160\n\\]\n\\[\n9x^2 = x^2 + 8x + 160\n\\]\nSubtract \\( x^2 \\) from both sides:\n\\[\n8x^2 = 8x + 160\n\\]\nDivide by 8:\n\\[\nx^2 = x + 20\n\\]\nRearrange to form a quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - x - 20 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -1 \\), and \\( c = -20 \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-(-1) \\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-20)}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 80}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1 \\pm 9}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{-8}{2} = -4\n\\]\n\nSince \\( x \\) represents a length, it must be positive:\n\\[\nx = 5\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( x = 5 \\) back to find \\( b \\):\n\\[\nb = 3x = 3 \\cdot 5 = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( AC \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
